"""Original worked examples, clearly separated from official restaurant figures."""
EXAMPLES={
'how-to-read-a-nutrition-label.html':'''<section class="publication-example" id="worked-comparison"><h2>Compare the portion, not just the label</h2><p>Imagine two yogurt labels. These are illustrative numbers, not specific products. Yogurt A lists 120 calories and 12 g protein per 150 g. Yogurt B lists 160 calories and 18 g protein per 200 g.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Both labels adjusted to a 200 g portion</caption><thead><tr><th>Food</th><th>Calories</th><th>Protein</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Yogurt A</th><td>120 × 200 ÷ 150 = 160</td><td>12 × 200 ÷ 150 = 16 g</td></tr><tr><th>Yogurt B</th><td>160</td><td>18 g</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The lower-calorie label did not mean fewer calories for the portion you wanted. At the same weight, the calories match and B has 2 g more protein. Price, taste and ingredients may still change your choice.</p><p><a href="nutrition-label-comparison-tool.html">Try this comparison with your own labels</a>. Serving sizes on U.S. labels describe amounts people typically consume; they are not a recommendation for how much you should eat. <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/nutrition-facts-label/how-understand-and-use-nutrition-facts-label">FDA label guidance</a>.</p></section>''',
'serving-size-vs-portion-size.html':'''<section class="publication-example" id="worked-comparison"><h2>A 30 g serving, a 45 g portion</h2><p>Suppose a cereal label lists 150 calories and 4 g protein per 30 g. You pour 45 g. Your multiplier is 45 ÷ 30 = 1.5, so the cereal contributes 225 calories and 6 g protein. Milk, yogurt and toppings are separate.</p><p>This arithmetic changes the amount recorded; it does not tell you whether 45 g is right for your appetite. If you use cups instead of weighing food, how tightly it is packed can change the estimate.</p><p><a href="recipe-macro-scaler.html">Calculate nutrition per recipe portion</a>, or <a href="nutrition-label-comparison-tool.html">compare two food labels</a>. These sample figures are invented only to demonstrate the calculation, not presented as food data.</p></section>''',
'how-many-calories-should-i-eat-a-day.html':'''<section class="publication-example" id="worked-comparison"><h2>What changes the calculator’s answer?</h2><p>For an illustrative 30-year-old man weighing 80 kg at 180 cm, the Mifflin–St Jeor equation gives 10 × 80 + 6.25 × 180 − 5 × 30 + 5 = 1,780 calories of estimated resting energy.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>The same person, two activity assumptions</caption><thead><tr><th>Assumption</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Estimated daily energy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Lower activity</th><td>1,780 × 1.2</td><td>2,136 calories</td></tr><tr><th>Moderate activity</th><td>1,780 × 1.55</td><td>2,759 calories</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>That 623-calorie difference comes from the activity assumption alone. The decimal places in a calculator cannot remove that uncertainty. Use the result as a starting estimate, then reconsider the assumption as your routine changes.</p><p><a href="calculators.html">Use the free macro calculator</a>. <a href="sources.html">Equation sources and calculator limitations</a>. This adult example is not a calorie prescription.</p></section>''',
'how-to-hit-protein-goal-on-budget.html':'''<section class="publication-example" id="worked-comparison"><h2>Which package actually costs less per gram?</h2><p>Use prices from the same shop and currency. These hypothetical prices illustrate the method; they are not current retail quotes.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Total protein</th><th>Cost per gram</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>A: $4, four servings with 10 g each</th><td>40 g</td><td>$4 ÷ 40 = $0.10</td></tr><tr><th>B: $6, five servings with 15 g each</th><td>75 g</td><td>$6 ÷ 75 = $0.08</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Package B has the higher checkout price but lower protein cost. That advantage matters only if you can afford the purchase and use the food. Include leftovers, preparation and the rest of the meal when deciding.</p><p><a href="protein-value-calculator.html">Compare your own package prices</a>. For canned food, use the label’s stated serving basis and the amount you will actually eat; do not mix drained weight with nutrition given for an undrained serving.</p></section>''',
'how-to-eat-out-without-wrecking-your-goal.html':'''<section class="publication-example" id="worked-comparison"><h2>Build the comparison around your whole order</h2><p>Start with the exact entrée, then write down each side, sauce and drink you expect to have. Compare that total with another complete order. An entrée-only number can be accurate and still describe only part of your meal.</p><p>For example, a hypothetical 450-calorie entrée, 180-calorie side and 120-calorie drink total 750 calories. Replacing the drink with water changes that example to 630; it does not change the entrée’s protein. These figures show the addition, not a published restaurant order.</p><ol><li>Check whether the listed nutrition includes dressing, sauce and standard toppings.</li><li>Compare meals from the same country’s menu.</li><li>Use the restaurant’s calculator for substitutions; do not assume removing a named ingredient subtracts a fixed amount.</li><li>Choose a meal that also fits your appetite, preferences and budget.</li></ol><p><a href="restaurant-meal-finder.html">Compare tracked restaurant meals</a>. Our guides identify calculated combinations and link to the official source so you can check the current order.</p></section>'''
}
